      "text": "To find the ratio of \\(AC\\) to \\(BD\\), we start by determining the lengths of the segments \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\(AB = 2\\) units\n- \\(BC = 5\\) units\n- \\(AD = 14\\) units\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of \\(AC\\). Since \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) are points on the line in that order, and \\(AC\\) is the sum of \\(AB\\) and \\(BC\\):\n\\[\nAC = AB + BC = 2 + 5 = 7 \\text{ units}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the length of \\(BD\\). Since \\(B\\) and \\(D\\) are points on the line in that order, and \\(BD\\) is the sum of \\(BC\\) and the part of \\(AD\\) that lies between \\(B\\) and \\(D\\) (which does not overlap with \\(BC\\)), we need to find the position of \\(D\\) relative to \\(B\\). Since \\(D\\) is a point on the line and \\(B\\) is between \\(A\\) and \\(C\\), \\(D\\) must be to the right of \\(B\\) by some distance \\(x\\), where \\(x\\) is the length of the remaining segment of \\(AD\\) after \\(BC\\). The remaining length of \\(AD\\) after \\(BC\\) is:\n\\[\nAD - BC = 14 - 5 = 9 \\text{ units}\n\\]\nSince \\(AC = 7\\) units, the remaining length \\(BD\\) is:\n\\[\nBD = BC + x = 5 + x\n\\]\nTo find \\(x\\), we need to determine where \\(D\\) is relative to \\(B\\). Since \\(A\\) is at the start and \\(C\\) is at a point \\(7\\) units to the right of \\(B\\), and \\(D\\) is \\(5\\) units to the right of \\(B\\), the distance \\(x\\) must be:\n\\[\nx = AD - AC = 14 - 7 = 7 \\text{ units}\n\\]\nTherefore, the length of \\(BD\\) is:\n\\[\nBD = 5 + x = 5 + 5 = 10 \\text{ units}\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the ratio of \\(AC\\) to \\(BD\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{BD} = \\frac{7}{10}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of \\(AC\\) to \\(BD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{10}}\n\\]",
